WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.583
SPEAKER: It's really easy to set
up autoscaling on your Google

00:00:02.583 --> 00:00:04.200
Cloud managed instance groups.

00:00:04.200 --> 00:00:06.840
The configuration options
offer lots of flexibility

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:09.190
to help fit your
application's needs.

00:00:09.190 --> 00:00:11.760
Let's dive a little deeper
into autoscaling and check out

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:14.580
three things that will help you
set up effective autoscaling

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.825
on your work instance groups.

00:00:22.170 --> 00:00:24.180
To implement more
effective autoscaling,

00:00:24.180 --> 00:00:27.060
it's helpful to understand
how it generally works.

00:00:27.060 --> 00:00:29.220
The autoscaler monitors
your instance group

00:00:29.220 --> 00:00:31.200
and offers a size
recommendation.

00:00:31.200 --> 00:00:32.820
That's a calculated
number of VMs

00:00:32.820 --> 00:00:36.000
needed to serve the peak load
over the last 10 minutes.

00:00:36.000 --> 00:00:39.540
These 10 minutes are referred
to as the stabilization period.

00:00:39.540 --> 00:00:41.830
This stabilization
period is 10 minutes,

00:00:41.830 --> 00:00:44.910
so the autoscaler can ensure
that usage info that it uses

00:00:44.910 --> 00:00:46.710
to scale in and out is stable.

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:48.600
And that helps
prevent the autoscaler

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.300
from adding and removing
machines at an excessive rate.

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:54.490
When scaling back down,
it's able to determine

00:00:54.490 --> 00:00:55.900
that a smaller
group size will be

00:00:55.900 --> 00:00:58.420
able to support the peak
load, so that it can safely

00:00:58.420 --> 00:01:00.280
remove instances.

00:01:00.280 --> 00:01:03.130
When autoscaling is turned
on the number of VMs

00:01:03.130 --> 00:01:05.140
it scales your instance
group up and down

00:01:05.140 --> 00:01:07.840
to will be limited by the
minimum and maximum number

00:01:07.840 --> 00:01:09.340
of VMs that you specify.

00:01:09.340 --> 00:01:12.370
When scaling in, the
number of VMs it can remove

00:01:12.370 --> 00:01:16.730
is also limited by the scale-in
controls, if specified.

00:01:16.730 --> 00:01:19.180
If you configure autoscaling
and leave it turned off,

00:01:19.180 --> 00:01:21.490
it still calculates
recommendations.

00:01:21.490 --> 00:01:23.230
So you can see what
the autoscaler would

00:01:23.230 --> 00:01:25.240
do if it's turned
on without having

00:01:25.240 --> 00:01:27.580
to spin up additional
machines in the process.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.380
This way, you can
see the effects

00:01:29.380 --> 00:01:31.732
it will have before
actually committing to them.

00:01:31.732 --> 00:01:33.940
So now that you know a little
bit about how it works,

00:01:33.940 --> 00:01:35.940
here are three things to
consider when designing

00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.450
your autoscaling configuration.

00:01:37.450 --> 00:01:40.300
Thing number one,
the machine type.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.130
OK, so technically
this isn't part

00:01:42.130 --> 00:01:43.660
of the autoscale configuration.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:45.340
You actually indicate
the machine type

00:01:45.340 --> 00:01:47.710
on the instance template
associated with your instance

00:01:47.710 --> 00:01:48.640
group.

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:51.070
But that's why it's so
important for autoscaling.

00:01:51.070 --> 00:01:52.750
A new instance of
that type will be

00:01:52.750 --> 00:01:55.360
provisioned when the
instance group is scaled up.

00:01:55.360 --> 00:01:57.760
You might be thinking,
well, yeah, of course.

00:01:57.760 --> 00:02:00.440
But this is when knowing
your application is key.

00:02:00.440 --> 00:02:03.070
For example, if you know that
your application that you're

00:02:03.070 --> 00:02:06.070
running on your instance
group needs two virtual CPUs,

00:02:06.070 --> 00:02:09.050
then e2-standard-2
has got you covered.

00:02:09.050 --> 00:02:11.980
But if you also know that
the same application doesn't

00:02:11.980 --> 00:02:15.190
need much memory,
then e2-highcpu-2

00:02:15.190 --> 00:02:19.420
offers the same number of
virtual CPUs with less memory.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:21.550
Choosing that one means
that, every time you

00:02:21.550 --> 00:02:23.440
scale your instance
group out, you're

00:02:23.440 --> 00:02:25.990
not scaling out or
paying for resources

00:02:25.990 --> 00:02:27.700
that you won't be using.

00:02:27.700 --> 00:02:30.880
OK, thing two, the
cool-down period.

00:02:30.880 --> 00:02:32.350
For effective
autoscaling, you want

00:02:32.350 --> 00:02:35.530
to make sure you specify an
accurate cool-down period.

00:02:35.530 --> 00:02:37.910
The cool-down period is
the initialization time,

00:02:37.910 --> 00:02:39.910
the amount of time it
takes for your application

00:02:39.910 --> 00:02:43.480
to initialize after the host
VM has been provisioned.

00:02:43.480 --> 00:02:46.400
You want to be sure that you
have an accurate one specified.

00:02:46.400 --> 00:02:49.180
If you specify a cool-down
period that's too long,

00:02:49.180 --> 00:02:51.790
then your autoscaler will miss
out on that utilization data

00:02:51.790 --> 00:02:53.740
and potentially
underestimate the required

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:55.210
size of your group.

00:02:55.210 --> 00:02:58.078
On the flip side, if you specify
a cool-down period that's

00:02:58.078 --> 00:03:00.370
shorter than it takes for
your application to be ready,

00:03:00.370 --> 00:03:02.170
then it's possible
for the autoscaler

00:03:02.170 --> 00:03:05.620
to get inaccurate utilization
data, because in some cases,

00:03:05.620 --> 00:03:08.710
CPU utilization while things
are getting up and running

00:03:08.710 --> 00:03:11.920
is higher and isn't a
reflection of normal use.

00:03:11.920 --> 00:03:15.790
Finally, there's thing
three, scaling controls.

00:03:15.790 --> 00:03:18.070
The ability for your managed
instance group to scale

00:03:18.070 --> 00:03:20.420
is a big win for reducing costs.

00:03:20.420 --> 00:03:22.420
But you don't want to
take a hit in performance

00:03:22.420 --> 00:03:23.590
when you do that.

00:03:23.590 --> 00:03:25.600
Scaling controls
let you slow things

00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:28.490
down by establishing a rate that
the autoscaler will scale down

00:03:28.490 --> 00:03:28.990
at.

00:03:28.990 --> 00:03:30.610
This way, you'll
be able to avoid

00:03:30.610 --> 00:03:32.360
dramatic drops in group size.

00:03:32.360 --> 00:03:35.020
Remember that cool-down period
I just mentioned before?

00:03:35.020 --> 00:03:37.450
If your cool-down period
is on the long side,

00:03:37.450 --> 00:03:40.210
by using scaling controls
you'll have the added benefit

00:03:40.210 --> 00:03:41.950
when there's a sudden
spike in demand.

00:03:41.950 --> 00:03:43.720
You'll already have
machines available

00:03:43.720 --> 00:03:46.990
and you can avoid delays
caused by long initializations.

00:03:46.990 --> 00:03:49.062
Setting up the right
scaling controls,

00:03:49.062 --> 00:03:51.520
just like choosing the right
machine and accurate cool-down

00:03:51.520 --> 00:03:52.960
period, all comes
down to knowing

00:03:52.960 --> 00:03:56.200
your application, its
requirements, and expected load

00:03:56.200 --> 00:03:57.530
and behavior.

00:03:57.530 --> 00:03:59.680
Let's take a look at this
managed instance group

00:03:59.680 --> 00:04:01.940
that I've set up on a
Google Cloud project.

00:04:01.940 --> 00:04:04.420
Here's a few things
about my configuration.

00:04:04.420 --> 00:04:08.410
I've set my minimum instances
to two and my maximum to 100.

00:04:08.410 --> 00:04:11.120
When I set this up, I wasn't
sure about cool-down period,

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:13.900
so I left it at the default
value of 60 seconds.

00:04:13.900 --> 00:04:15.650
I wasn't sure about
scaling controls,

00:04:15.650 --> 00:04:17.500
so I left that disabled.

00:04:17.500 --> 00:04:20.680
The instance template
uses an e2-standard-2.

00:04:20.680 --> 00:04:23.500
Let's see how we can make
changes to this configuration.

00:04:23.500 --> 00:04:25.390
Let's edit my managed
instance group called

00:04:25.390 --> 00:04:29.557
web-app-instance-group and check
out the autoscale settings.

00:04:29.557 --> 00:04:31.390
We've got a regional-managed
instance group.

00:04:31.390 --> 00:04:33.100
So right off the bat, I
want to take advantage

00:04:33.100 --> 00:04:34.660
of high availability
by making sure

00:04:34.660 --> 00:04:37.780
that I've got at least one
instance in each of my zones.

00:04:37.780 --> 00:04:41.388
That's three zones, so I'll
increase my minimum to three.

00:04:41.388 --> 00:04:43.180
Now that I'm here, I
also want to make sure

00:04:43.180 --> 00:04:46.180
that my maximum is set
to a reasonable number.

00:04:46.180 --> 00:04:48.220
Although it may be
unlikely for my application

00:04:48.220 --> 00:04:50.770
to need to scale that high,
it's still possible for it

00:04:50.770 --> 00:04:52.720
to go as high as 100.

00:04:52.720 --> 00:04:54.740
For my application,
this is too much.

00:04:54.740 --> 00:04:56.293
So let's lower that to 30.

00:04:56.293 --> 00:04:58.210
It takes the application
on my virtual machine

00:04:58.210 --> 00:04:59.390
two minutes to be ready.

00:04:59.390 --> 00:05:03.620
So I'll set the cool
down to 120 seconds.

00:05:03.620 --> 00:05:04.430
That's pretty long.

00:05:04.430 --> 00:05:06.680
So I'm going to enable
scaling controls in case

00:05:06.680 --> 00:05:09.710
I get any spikes when my
group is scaling down.

00:05:09.710 --> 00:05:12.020
In this case, my instance
group won't scale down

00:05:12.020 --> 00:05:15.410
by more than 10 VMs from
the peak size, as observed

00:05:15.410 --> 00:05:17.733
over the prior 10 minutes.

00:05:17.733 --> 00:05:19.400
I'm going to save
these settings for now

00:05:19.400 --> 00:05:21.380
before I change one more thing.

00:05:21.380 --> 00:05:25.130
The VM type specified in the
template is an e2-standard-2.

00:05:25.130 --> 00:05:27.190
Well, that's got more
memory than I need.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.680
So let's see how I can replace
that with an e2-highcpu-2

00:05:30.680 --> 00:05:32.230
instead.

00:05:32.230 --> 00:05:34.300
Rather than clicking Edit
on my instance group,

00:05:34.300 --> 00:05:37.870
I can instead go up
here to Update VMs.

00:05:37.870 --> 00:05:40.987
Up top here, it says the current
size of my instance group

00:05:40.987 --> 00:05:42.445
and the name of
the template I used

00:05:42.445 --> 00:05:44.170
to create the instances in it.

00:05:44.170 --> 00:05:47.023
Here, under New Template, I
could add a second template

00:05:47.023 --> 00:05:48.940
and roll that out to
only a certain percentage

00:05:48.940 --> 00:05:50.630
of my instance group.

00:05:50.630 --> 00:05:52.540
This is one way
to do A/B testing

00:05:52.540 --> 00:05:54.730
or deploy a canary
of my application.

00:05:54.730 --> 00:05:57.640
In this case, I want to
replace my only template

00:05:57.640 --> 00:06:00.310
with a different one that I
created for this demonstration.

00:06:00.310 --> 00:06:02.620
With one template
specified, my target size

00:06:02.620 --> 00:06:04.810
is 100% of my instance group.

00:06:04.810 --> 00:06:07.060
Down here, under
Update Configuration,

00:06:07.060 --> 00:06:10.210
I can specify how I'd like
the template to be rolled out.

00:06:10.210 --> 00:06:12.730
By selecting Automatic,
the managed instance group

00:06:12.730 --> 00:06:14.440
will proactively
tear down or replace

00:06:14.440 --> 00:06:16.000
each of my instance
groups instances

00:06:16.000 --> 00:06:17.650
created with the old template.

00:06:17.650 --> 00:06:20.740
Here, where it says Temporary
Additional Instances,

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.500
I can specify how many machines
beyond the recommended number

00:06:23.500 --> 00:06:25.917
can be provisioned to make
this transition possible.

00:06:25.917 --> 00:06:28.000
This makes it possible to
maintain the recommended

00:06:28.000 --> 00:06:30.640
number of VMs while still
tearing down and recreating

00:06:30.640 --> 00:06:31.510
them.

00:06:31.510 --> 00:06:33.430
Down here at the bottom
is an update summary

00:06:33.430 --> 00:06:36.130
that recaps the configuration
I set for this update.

00:06:36.130 --> 00:06:38.380
Once I'm finished, I
can kick this update

00:06:38.380 --> 00:06:42.850
off using the equivalent
command line or REST API calls.

00:06:42.850 --> 00:06:45.970
Otherwise, I can just
click Update VMs here.

00:06:45.970 --> 00:06:48.400
The autoscaling offered
of managed instance groups

00:06:48.400 --> 00:06:50.150
is very flexible.

00:06:50.150 --> 00:06:51.970
The cool-down period
and scaling controls

00:06:51.970 --> 00:06:54.970
are a great start to setting
up effective autoscaling.

00:06:54.970 --> 00:06:57.610
Also, don't forget to
choose a properly sized VM

00:06:57.610 --> 00:06:58.960
on your instance template.

00:06:58.960 --> 00:07:00.520
Beyond that, there's
much more you

00:07:00.520 --> 00:07:02.500
can learn from the
documentation to make sure

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:05.180
that your autoscaling is
working for you efficiently,

00:07:05.180 --> 00:07:06.670
effectively, and affordably.

00:07:06.670 --> 00:07:09.443
To learn more, check out this
link for more on autoscaling.

00:07:09.443 --> 00:07:11.860
And check out the link in the
description for another demo

00:07:11.860 --> 00:07:13.480
on autoscaling.

00:07:13.480 --> 00:07:15.390
Thanks for watching.

